  the crossover -- where the kernel's anomalous tide equals the Galactic vertical tide -- is at r = 498 (canonical) / 452 AU.  Because the switch-on is exponential, a factor 2
  of theory error in A moves this radius by only ~4%, which is why the number is worth quoting at all.
  [PASS] 40b the framework's real statement about the outer solar system is a SHARP APHELION SCALE, not a perihelion one: because Route A's return to Newton is exponential (nu-1 = exp(-r_M/r) exactly), the kernel's anomalous tide is switched on over less than a factor 3 in radius, and it overtakes the Galactic tide at ~450-500 AU.  A decade inside that it is 10^-100 of the Galaxy's; a factor 6 outside it is 10^4 times   (crossover 498 / 452 AU; log10(A_MOND/A_gal) = -4.1 at 300 AU, 4.3 at 3000 AU)
  
  BUG FOUND AND FIXED IN THE MAKING: the first version of this script used sin^2 of the ECLIPTIC inclination as the
  Galactic tide's efficiency factor.  That is wrong -- the vertical tide's axis is the Galactic pole, which is 60 deg
  from the ecliptic pole -- and it made the Newtonian alternative look about 5x weaker than it is.  Corrected here:
  Sedna's ecliptic inclination is 11.9 deg (sin^2 = 0.04) but its inclination to the GALACTIC plane gives sin^2 = 0.85.

