  [PASS] 40f the kernel's exponential return is what keeps the framework out of the planetary ephemerides: Route A's anomalous quadrupole at Saturn is exp(-r_M/r)-suppressed to 10^-380, where the same expansion with a power-law-return kernel lands within an order of magnitude of the published limit.  This is the outer-solar-system face of the repository's standing alpha = 1 ephemeris liability and it comes out the right way for Route A   (Route A at Saturn 10^-380 s^-2 vs the bound 3e-27; the alpha=1 form at the same place is 2.4e-28)

====================================================================================================================
the signature that is the framework's alone: the tide's symmetry axis
====================================================================================================================
  the Galactic vertical tide is symmetric about the GALACTIC POLE; the kernel's anomalous tide is symmetric about the
  direction of the external field, i.e. about the SUN-GALACTIC-CENTRE line.  Two different axes, so the two mechanisms
  sculpt different orientation patterns.  Nothing in Newtonian gravity has the second axis at all.
  the 44 extreme objects                 N =    44: <cos^2(normal, GC)> = 0.063 +- 0.009, <cos^2(normal, NGP)> = 0.222 +- 0.027   (isotropic = 0.333)
  classical belt (the selection control) N =  3462: <cos^2(normal, GC)> = 0.046 +- 0.001, <cos^2(normal, NGP)> = 0.232 +- 0.002   (isotropic = 0.333)
  [PASS] 40g UNDERPOWERED, and recorded as underpowered rather than as a null: the orientation test that would separate the kernel's tide (axis = the Galactic centre) from the Galactic tide (axis = the pole) needs the extreme objects' orbit normals to sit differently about the GC direction than a selection-matched control does.  With 44 objects the difference is under 2 sigma against a 3 sigma bar, and the ecliptic survey footprint dominates both samples.  This is the right test to inherit and it needs LSST's ETNO haul, not this one   (<cos^2 to GC> = 0.063 +- 0.009 for the extreme objects vs 0.046 +- 0.001 for the classical belt -> 1.8 sigma; both far from isotropic (0.333) because both are ecliptic-selected)

  [PASS] M40 mutation: a_0 -> 100 a_0 shrinks r_M tenfold and must move the crossover radius by the same order -- if it does not, the exponential is not doing the work and the estimator is broken   (crossover moves 498 -> 34 AU under a_0 x100)
  [PASS] M40b mutation: shuffling the ratios between objects destroys the raw correlation, confirming the estimator reads the pairing and not the marginals -- which is exactly why the pairing being forced by (a, e) makes the test void   (2000 shuffles: mean |rho| = 0.121, 0.0% exceed the real |0.521|)
  [PASS] M40c mutation: with no external field the whole anomalous tide must vanish identically -- the effect is the EXTERNAL FIELD acting through the kernel, which is exactly why Newtonian gravity has no counterpart to it   (x_ext = 0 gives A = 0.0e+00 s^-2 where x_ext = 1.66 gives 4.3e-27)

  SUMMARY OF ITEM 40, both ways.  The perihelion-edge-at-r_M prediction the list asked for does not exist and is
  withdrawn with its numbers.  The framework's genuine outer-solar-system statement is an exponentially sharp
  APHELION crossover at ~450-500 AU beyond which its tide dwarfs the Galaxy's by four orders of magnitude -- and the
  detached population cannot resolve it: the median perihelion IS higher above the edge as the framework wants
  (p = 0.05), but the three most detached bodies known sit BELOW it where the mechanism does nothing (p = 0.09), and
  the correlation route is void because q, a and the tide ratio are all functions of the same two orbital elements.
  Planet 9, a stellar flyby and the birth cluster are not displaced by this.  What is worth inheriting is the
  orientation test: the kernel's tide has a symmetry axis pointing at the Galactic centre and Newtonian gravity does not.

